       "evidence": "PHASE 12 POINT 8 — additive repair proven necessary by the clean-artifact rehearsal. 20260620100000_phase24_31_consolidated_drift_patches creates evidence_search_documents.embedding and its IVFFLAT index inside IF has_pgvector, but CREATE EXTENSION vector is issued by 20270701000000_phase15_semantic_search, a year later in lexical order. On any database built from this chain the guard is false when evaluated and both objects are silently skipped, while the datamodel DECLARES the column (EvidenceSearchDocument.embedding Unsupported(\"vector(384)\")?) and raw-schema-ownership.json registers the index. Applying the artifact to an empty PostgreSQL 16 + pgvector and running db:raw-schema-verify reported the index gone. Every statement here is conditional and idempotent, so it is a no-op on production, which acquired the objects out of band. It RAISES rather than skipping when pgvector is absent, because the silent skip is what hid this.",
       "rollbackDisposition": "Additive only. DROP INDEX IF EXISTS evidence_search_documents_embedding_ivfflat; the column is declared by the datamodel and should not be dropped."
+    },
+    "20271120000000_external_review_delivery_intent_idempotency": {
+      "classification": "EXPAND",
+      "releaseWave": "SAFE_TO_APPLY_NOW",
+      "safeBeforeCodeDeployment": true,
+      "evidence": "PHASE 12 CORRECTIVE PASS §1.1 (2026-08-06) — the missing half of the invitation-email idempotency contract, found by runtime probing rather than by tests. sendInvitationEmail created a NEW external_review_invitation_deliveries row on every call and derived the provider idempotency key from that row id, so a retry minted a second key and the external reviewer received the invitation twice; four concurrent sends minted four. The application half (reuse-on-conflict, portal-invitation-email.service.ts#reserveDeliveryIntent) cannot be correct without a database constraint, because check-then-insert races. This migration adds UNIQUE (team_id, grant_id, attempt) and, first, deterministically re-numbers any pre-existing duplicate attempts (oldest row keeps its position; history is renumbered, never deleted, because delivery history is audit-relevant). Both statements are wrapped in information_schema column-existence guards, so the migration is a no-op where the table or a referenced column is absent. Classified EXPAND / SAFE_TO_APPLY_NOW: it adds a constraint and drops nothing, and the application half tolerates its absence (it simply does not collapse) so it is safe to apply before the code deploys. Proven at runtime by services/api/test/phase-12-sec-001-completion.integration.test.ts (9/9 against disposable PostgreSQL 16 + pgvector), whose retry, four-way-concurrency and idempotency-key probes all failed before it and pass after.",
+      "rollbackDisposition": "Additive only. DROP INDEX IF EXISTS \"external_review_invitation_deliveries_grant_attempt_key\"; the attempt re-numbering is not reversed (and need not be — it only compacts attempt numbers within a grant)."
     }
